{
  "assessment": "The source provides a perturbative expression for the inverse localization length in the 1D Anderson model with stealthy hyperuniform disorder, including first- and second-order coefficients, and numerical confirmation of scaling exponents. The best quantitative cluster is the localization length scaling with disorder strength W for fixed energy E=-1.8 and various stealthiness chi, with two regimes: chi=0.1 giving xi~W^-2 and chi=0.2,0.25,0.3 giving xi~W^-4. The first-order coefficient is given explicitly; the second-order coefficient is given as an integral and an expansion near chi=1/2. The numerical data confirm the scaling but do not provide a closed-form coefficient for the W^-4 regime. Thus, the extraction is case_authoring_ready with the first-order relation as the primary quantitative claim, and the second-order scaling as a secondary claim with a symbolic coefficient.",
  "case_authoring_ready": true,
  "source": {
    "citation": "Carlo Vanoni, Effective Delocalization in the One-Dimensional Anderson Model with Stealthy Disorder, Physical Review Letters (2026), DOI:10.1103/nl7n-bqn4",
    "journal_doi": "10.1103/nl7n-bqn4",
    "arxiv_versioned_id": "2509.13502v3"
  },
  "paper_scope": "The paper studies the 1D Anderson model with stealthy hyperuniform disorder, computing the localization length via perturbative self-energy expansion and numerical diagonalization. It focuses on the scaling of localization length with disorder strength W and stealthiness parameter chi, showing effective delocalization for finite systems.",
  "mechanisms": [
    {
      "mechanism_id": "first_order_self_energy",
      "statement": "First-order perturbative self-energy contribution to the inverse localization length for stealthy hyperuniform disorder, valid when S(2k) is nonzero.",
      "equations": [
        "1/xi(k) = W^2/(8 sin^2 k) S(2k)"
      ],
      "variable_names": ["xi", "W", "k", "S", "k0", "chi"],
      "parameters": [
        {
          "name": "first_order_coeff",
          "value": 0.125,
          "uncertainty": "source_unspecified",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "coefficient in 1/xi = coeff * W^2 * S(2k) / sin^2(k)",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 258,
              "line_end": 261,
              "support": "Equation (eq:first-order) gives 1/xi(k) = W^2/(8 sin^2 k) S(2k), so the coefficient is 1/8."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 258,
          "line_end": 261,
          "support": "Equation (eq:first-order) states 1/xi(k) = W^2/(8 sin^2 k) S(2k)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "mechanism_id": "second_order_self_energy",
      "statement": "Second-order perturbative self-energy contribution to the inverse localization length, valid when the first-order term vanishes (2k < k0 < pi - k).",
      "equations": [
        "1/xi(k) = W^4/(32 pi sin^2(k)) * [ ... ]",
        "1/xi(k) ~ W^4/(32 pi sin^2(k)) * (pi-k0)/2 * sec^2(k) for k0 near pi"
      ],
      "variable_names": ["xi", "W", "k", "k0", "chi"],
      "parameters": [
        {
          "name": "second_order_coeff_near_pi",
          "value": 0.03125,
          "uncertainty": "source_unspecified",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "coefficient in 1/xi ~ coeff * W^4 * (pi-k0) * sec^2(k) / sin^2(k) for k0 near pi",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 535,
              "line_end": 539,
              "support": "Expansion around k0=pi gives 1/xi = W^4/(32 pi sin^2(k)) * (pi-k0)/2 * sec^2(k) + ...; the leading coefficient is 1/(64 pi) times (pi-k0) sec^2(k)/sin^2(k)."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 523,
          "line_end": 534,
          "support": "Equation (eq:second-order) gives the full second-order inverse localization length expression."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 535,
          "line_end": 539,
          "support": "Expansion near k0=pi gives leading term proportional to (pi-k0) sec^2(k)."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
